{
  "query": "You are the database engineer for a social fitness startup that expects roughly 90 million user submissions per month that include text comments, image metadata, and video references. The team requires low-latency access from mobile and web clients and wants a flexible schema that can change without planned downtime. Which Google Cloud database service should you choose?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Cloud Bigtable",
      "explanation": "A wide column NoSQL store that is optimized for massive scale and high throughput for analytical and operational workloads",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Firestore",
      "explanation": "A serverless document database that supports mobile, web, and backend applications with flexible schemas and real time access",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Cloud Spanner",
      "explanation": "A globally distributed relational database that provides strong consistency and horizontal scaling for transactional workloads",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "AlloyDB for PostgreSQL",
      "explanation": "A fully managed PostgreSQL compatible relational service that is designed for high performance enterprise transactional use cases",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Firestore</b>.</p><p><b>Firestore</b> is a serverless document database that gives you a flexible schema and it lets you evolve your data model without planned downtime. It provides low latency reads and writes from web and mobile clients and offers rich SDKs with real time listeners and offline sync which fits a social app that records text comments, image metadata, and video references. It scales seamlessly to handle about 90 million user submissions per month while keeping development simple with security rules and automatic indexing.</p><p><i>Cloud Bigtable</i> is optimized for extremely high throughput and wide column workloads such as time series or IoT and it does not offer rich querying, secondary indexes, or client SDKs with offline sync for web and mobile apps. Modeling user centric access patterns is harder and it lacks features that make rapid application development easy for this use case, so it is not the best fit here.</p><p><i>Cloud Spanner</i> is a horizontally scalable relational database that requires a predefined schema and schema changes through DDL, and while those changes are online they still add operational overhead compared with a schema flexible document store. It excels when you need relational joins and strong consistency across many rows, but it is unnecessary complexity and cost for this workload that mainly needs flexible content and low latency access from clients.</p><p><i>AlloyDB for PostgreSQL</i> delivers high performance for relational workloads but it also requires a fixed schema and migrations and it primarily scales using replicas and larger nodes rather than effortless serverless scaling. It does not provide native real time sync or offline ready client SDKs for web and mobile, so it is less suitable for rapidly evolving social app data.</p>",
